  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at 0.4 × loss over x, z from -2 to 2. Steps overshoot in x when the learning rate is above 1/3. Swings grow when it is above 2/3, as at 1.",
    "Noise is added to each slope component with spread 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b. That is 2 at b = 1, about 0.98 at 4, and exactly 0 at 64 (all points). A step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Arrows are 3 × the true step (learning rate × slope estimate). Orange is the mini-batch step the ball takes next. Grey is the full-data step. At 0 they are hidden and the length reads 0.",
    "A run ends as finished after 40 steps, or as diverged once the loss passes 6. Positions past the bowl's edge are drawn at the edge, and the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The ball moves one step every 6 host ticks (5 per second at speed 1). 'Step once' makes one update. Reset replays the same noise sequence from the seed."
  ]
}